                <h2>Applicable Scenarios and Prerequisites</h2>
<p>This article is suitable for the cold start phase of new products on TikTok Shop in US, UK, or Southeast Asia markets. When your product has just been listed, your account has a weak follower base, and organic traffic hasn't picked up yet, you need to quickly test materials through paid traffic and accumulate conversion data.</p>
<p><strong>Prerequisites:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Product listing completed with basic detail pages</li>
<li>Advertising account activated and initial deposit made</li>
<li>Available shooting resources or UGC creator resources</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>Step 1: Product Analysis to Lock Material Direction</h2>
<p>Before shooting videos, you must clarify "what to sell" and "how to sell it." This step determines the correctness of your subsequent material direction.</p>
<h3>1.1 Extract Core Product Selling Points</h3>
<p>Rank product selling points by conversion priority. An operations team from a home storage brand shared their experience: they correspond "space-saving," "easy to clean," and "high appearance" to three content angles—practical testing, unboxing showcase, and lifestyle scene integration. Don't cram all selling points into one video.</p>
<h3>1.2 Identify Target Audience Pain Points</h3>
<p>Through comment crawling, competitor ad analysis, and TikTok Search trending keywords, identify the real needs of target users.</p>
<p><strong>Action Checklist:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Search core category keywords and count high-frequency requests in top comments</li>
<li>Follow 3-5 competing brand accounts and record types of user questions in comments</li>
<li>Use platform search box suggestions to judge search volume trends</li>
</ul>
<h3>1.3 Develop Material Angle Matrix</h3>
<p>Based on cross-analysis of selling points and pain points, produce 3-5 differentiated material angles. For example: pain point solution (showing before/after comparisons), scenario immersion (simulating real usage scenarios), and comparative testing (objective comparison with common market solutions).</p>
<hr />
<h2>Step 2: Material Type Selection and Production Standards</h2>
<p>During the cold start phase, focus on rapidly producing large quantities of testable materials rather than pursuing refined content, then screen for potential winners.</p>
<h3>2.1 Recommended Material Type Ratio</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Material Type</th>
<th>Recommended Ratio</th>
<th>Applicable Phase</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>UGC voiceover/recommendation</td>
<td>40%</td>
<td>Cold start main force</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Product unboxing/showcase</td>
<td>25%</td>
<td>Building trust</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Pain point scenario demo</td>
<td>20%</td>
<td>Improving conversion intent</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Factory/warehouse traceability</td>
<td>10%</td>
<td>Building brand authenticity</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Limited-time countdown</td>
<td>5%</td>
<td>With promotion events</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>2.2 Basic Video Production Standards</h3>
<p><strong>Dimensions and Duration:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Vertical 9:16 format primarily (1080×1920)</li>
<li>Core hook must appear in the first 3 seconds, avoid ineffective openings</li>
<li>Total duration controlled at 15-45 seconds, prioritize concise content during cold start</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Visual and Audio:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Opening brightness 20% higher than overall, quickly grab attention</li>
<li>Voiceover pace maintained at 180-200 words per minute with appropriate pauses</li>
<li>Background music selected from top 20 trending BGM list</li>
</ul>
<h3>2.3 Script Framework Template (Ready to Use)</h3>
<p><strong>Pain Point-Solution-Result Type:</strong></p>
<blockquote>
<p>"Did you ever encounter <strong><em>_</em>_? (Pain point opening, 2 seconds)<br />
I used to </strong><strong><em> until I tried this </em></strong><strong><em>. (Solution introduction, 5 seconds)<br />
Look, now </em></strong>__ — it's really good! (Result showcase, 10-15 seconds)<br />
Link in comments, click below to purchase." (Call to action, 2 seconds)</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>Step 3: Testing and Data Screening</h2>
<p>After material production, enter the actual advertising verification phase. The core of this step is "fail fast, eliminate decisively."</p>
<h3>3.1 Test Budget Allocation Principles</h3>
<p>During the cold start phase, recommended test budget per material is $50-100 with a testing period of 24-48 hours. Focus on the first 3-second retention rate and CTR, don't draw conclusions too early.</p>
<p><strong>Budget Allocation Reference:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>First batch test: 10-15 materials</li>
<li>Single material budget: $50-80</li>
<li>Prioritize testing UGC voiceover and pain point scenario types</li>
</ul>
<h3>3.2 Data Metric Priorities</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Metric</th>
<th>Target Standard</th>
<th>Action</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>CTR (Click-through rate)</td>
<td>&gt;1.5%</td>
<td>Prioritize scaling</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>3-second retention rate</td>
<td>&gt;45%</td>
<td>Optimize opening</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CVR (Conversion rate)</td>
<td>&gt;1%</td>
<td>Optimize landing page</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>CPA (Cost per acquisition)</td>
<td>&lt;30% of product price</td>
<td>Evaluate ROI</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>3.3 Material Elimination and Scaling SOP</h3>
<ol>
<li>After testing period, screen top 30% CTR materials for retesting</li>
<li>Increase single material budget to $200-300 in retest phase, observe stability</li>
<li>Materials with consecutive two days CPA below threshold and conversion volume &gt;10 are identified as potential materials</li>
<li>Potential materials enter mass production phase, batch-produce variants by similar structure</li>
<li>Materials with CTR below 0.8% and CVR below 0.5% are directly eliminated without repeated adjustments</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>Step 4: Material Library Maintenance and Iterative Optimization</h2>
<p>The material pool isn't complete after construction—it needs continuous updates to support long-term advertising.</p>
<h3>4.1 Regular Update Mechanism</h3>
<ul>
<li>Add 5-10 new materials to test pool weekly</li>
<li>Review material lifecycle monthly, replace declining materials</li>
<li>Reserve themed materials 2 weeks before holidays and major promotions</li>
</ul>
<h3>4.2 Viral Material Reuse Techniques</h3>
<p>The script structure of successful materials can be broken down into four modules: "opening hook + product showcase + usage demo + call to action." Extract the best-performing modules individually and combine new elements to produce variants:</p>
<ul>
<li>Change on-camera person</li>
<li>Adjust product color/model on display</li>
<li>Switch to different trending BGM</li>
<li>Vary voiceover delivery style</li>
</ul>
<h3>4.3 Follow Platform Content Trends</h3>
<p>TikTok algorithm preferences continuously evolve. Recommend operations teams set fixed weekly time to check Discover page trending topics and challenges, promptly leverage trends to produce relevant material content. A pet supplies seller once rode the "cat funny moments" topic, achieving a single material with stable ROI of 3.5.</p>
<hr />
<h2>Quick Troubleshooting</h2>
<p><strong>Q: What if there aren't enough materials during cold start?</strong></p>
<p>Prioritize ensuring 10 testable materials, then quickly expand to 20 through editing variants (adjusting clip order, replacing background music) to reduce per-unit production cost.</p>
<p><strong>Q: How to troubleshoot when test period data is generally poor?</strong></p>
<p>First check landing page load speed and purchase flow smoothness, then verify if material opening matches target audience, finally confirm if ad targeting isn't too narrow.</p>
<p><strong>Q: What if suitable creators for UGC materials can't be found?</strong></p>
<p>Try finding creators with TikTok content experience on platforms like Fiverr or Upwork, or directly invite mid-tier and lower-tier creators through the platform creator marketplace to reduce collaboration costs while acquiring native-feeling content.</p>
